msg.reasoning_content:
errReader is currently: `if e.n >= e.fail { return 0, errors.New("boom") }`. fail=2: calls 0, 1 are normal, and from call 2 onward boom. bufio's first Read call reads the entire string (strings.Reader returns the complete 30-byte string in one go, n=30, err=nil), then the second call returns 0, io.EOF — boom never happens. Because bufio fills just once. fail=2 means that at the point of the 2nd call boom is triggered, but… wait, call 1 (n=0): 30 bytes + EOF read? strings.Reader.Read: reads everything, err=nil. Call 2 (n=1): n=0, err=io.EOF. n<2 so no problem. Call 3 (n=2): boom! However, bufio's fill() loop: after the first Read returns n=30 err=nil, it returns. After the 2nd read (0, io.EOF) returns, b.err=io.EOF. So call 3 never happens — bufio stops filling. That's why boom never occurs.

A better approach: an errReader that fails after returning some data: for example, on the first call returns a prefix, and on the 2nd call fails. A custom reader that returns part of the string each time, and fails after the first call:

```go
type errReader struct {
	data string
	off  int
}

func (e *errReader) Read(p []byte) (int, error) {
	if e.off >= len(e.data) {
		return 0, io.EOF
	}
	n := copy(p, e.data[e.off:e.off+1]) // 1 byte per read
	e.off += n
	return n, nil
}
```

Hmm, this is just slow. Instead: a reader that serves up to N bytes before failing:

```go
type errReader struct {
	data  string
	off   int
	limit int // serve this many bytes, then fail
}
func (e *errReader) Read(p []byte) (int, error) {
	remaining := e.limit - e.off
	if remaining <= 0 {
		return 0, errors.New("boom")
	}
	if remaining > len(e.data)-e.off {
		remaining = len(e.data) - e.off
	}
	if remaining > len(p) {
		remaining = len(p)
	}
	n := copy(p, e.data[e.off:e.off+remaining])
	e.off += n
	return n, nil
}
```

With limit=10, the first read returns 10 bytes, and the next call fails with boom (not EOF). bufio will propagate this.

But wait — will bufio return an error after reading 10 bytes? My readRune: rr.peekRune -> br.ReadRune -> fill -> Read returns 10, nil. Then the run gets decoded. Later when the buffer is exhausted, fill reads -> boom -> err gets saved; ReadRune: b.r==b.w? After consuming 10 bytes, r==w, fill gets called: the first Read is… no, this is the 2nd Read call -> immediately boom. readErr() returns boom. Good, readErr=boom != io.EOF so it propagates.

Also, does strings.Reader return all data in a single Read regardless of len(p)? strings.Reader.Read: "Read implements Reader.Read; it returns the number of bytes read, and if n < len(p), err will be EOF." Actually it copies min(len(p), remaining), and err = nil if it got everything? strings.Reader: "subsequent calls return 0 and io.EOF". So the first Read returns everything (30 bytes) with err=nil, and boom doesn't trigger. My custom reader fixes this.

